WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.790
Dr. Ken Atchity, Producer/Author: Diversity
has always been something that I’ve been

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:09.420
in love with because when I was in college
I took English and Classics kind of double

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:15.299
major because I couldn’t decide which one
to go for.

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:22.060
When I heard about comparative literature
my last year at Georgetown I thought “This

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.210
is perfect, it’s the study of different
cultures.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.519
And it’s about putting things together.”

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:35.350
So I’ve written crazy pieces like comparing
Wallace Stevens to Petrarca and Dante with

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:41.520
Joyce and that’s what I think is the most
interesting thing is when you juxtapose two

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.540
things or three things rather than focusing
one thing.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:53.910
That’s one of the things where the academic
world annoyed me about sometimes until I discovered

00:00:53.910 --> 00:01:00.450
comparative literature and took that degree
at Yale and ended up teaching that and I think

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:06.790
that the rest of my life has been an extension
of working in more than one discipline.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:11.190
In addition to movies, I’m very involved
in books.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:18.180
I’ve just finished another book of my own
and I publish books because four years ago

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:25.580
I realized I was having a tough time after
selling books to New York for 20 years or

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:38.190
more and having more than 20 New York Times
Best Sellers

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:45.310
purchased by conglomerations around the world
there was not much chance for a young, new

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:52.190
voice, what they were looking for is established
brands and you know the old joke that Stephen

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.909
King could publish the phone book with his
name on it and that’s just the way it is

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:02.200
in this huge country where marketing and branding
is what it’s all about to get the attention

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.510
of this 300 million audience.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:10.720
So I came up with the idea and what was happening
to me is because I couldn’t publish things

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:16.790
as easily as I could before I published 250
books, and sold them to publishers and I could

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.480
take those books to Hollywood and sell them
to Hollywood.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:25.530
They no longer had books to take to Hollywood
so I decided to start my own imprint (Story

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:32.090
Merchant Books) which I did and that was five
years ago and we’ve published over 200 books

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:33.120
now.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:41.120
And I now take them to Hollywood and set them
up as series or set them up as movies and

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.990
no one seems to mind that they are not Random
House books.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.790
They don’t even look at the publisher basically.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:51.170
They listen to me pitch the story at lunch
and then they take the book home and read

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.170
it.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:59.870
And so I’ve always been involved in sort
of comparative situations because I’ve got

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:06.120
New York publishing and Hollywood and I’ve
used them back and forth against each other

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.770
because Hollywood has this huge respect for
books and New York has this awe of movies

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:18.319
primarily because of the marketing money associated
with movies they can then ride along with

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:20.980
when they publish the book.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:29.470
We are having a big auction on a book that
is appearing this summer after 22 years called

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:36.530
THE MEG and we sold it 22 years ago, that’s
how long it’s been.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:41.630
We developed it through one of my companies
and then sold it to a publisher and then we

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:48.310
sold it to the studio and it’s been 22 years
in development hell until finally it’s getting

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.420
made.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.879
And one of the good things about stories is
that they’re timeless.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:58.760
One of my favorite examples which is no solace
to its author Herman Melville is that Moby

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:06.459
Dick sold about 60 copies prior to Melville’s
death and within two decades after his death

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:12.489
it became not only an international bestseller
but The Great American Novel.

00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:16.949
So the stories are timeless and that’s what
writers are capable of doing, of creating

00:04:16.949 --> 00:04:21.539
something timeless which is of immense value
obviously to the human race.

00:04:21.539 --> 00:04:26.979
The Iliad and the Odyssey were composed thousands
of years ago and yet there are still on every

00:04:26.979 --> 00:04:31.800
bookstores shelves, you can find them all
over the Internet.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.570
So the power of stories I’ve always loved
the fact stories can grow up to be a book

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.120
or the same story can grow up and be a movie.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:51.139
Once I had a screenplay that I loved that
won the Nicholls award, one of the highest

00:04:51.139 --> 00:04:57.669
awards in the business and it was placed almost
at the top (not quite).

00:04:57.669 --> 00:05:01.629
But I read it, loved it, tried to sell it
to the studios.

00:05:01.629 --> 00:05:05.750
They all thought it was too original, the
didn’t know who the author was and they

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.479
ended up passing.

00:05:07.479 --> 00:05:11.360
So I told the author “Let’s turn this
story into a book.”

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.979
And so long story short, we did and I sold
it to a major publisher and within a couple

00:05:16.979 --> 00:05:20.990
of weeks of selling it to a major publisher,
we had an auction in Hollywood and sold it

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:24.830
to a major studio and it went into development.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:29.900
And we’ve done it kind of the opposite way
as someone was trying to write a project in

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:34.900
one form and I told them to write it in the
other because of a random conversation with

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:40.460
an editor and she thought it sounded like
the best novel she’d ever heard and he wasn’t

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:46.039
a novelist but long story short within three
months he wrote a novel, we sold it and then

00:05:46.039 --> 00:05:52.509
sold it for 1.2 million dollars to a major
studio in Hollywood.

00:05:52.509 --> 00:05:56.819
So I thought “That’s my comparative literature
background.

00:05:56.819 --> 00:06:03.819
The idea of having two different worlds to
put together and recognize how they are related

00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:05.960
to each other.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:13.210
I learned that there are wormholes between
New York and LA because when I was just beginning

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:21.849
to shop the novel in the second case I send
it to only one publisher which was the person

00:06:21.849 --> 00:06:27.460
who told me it sounded like a great idea (she
didn’t know I only sent it to her, but I

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:33.999
sent it to her alone) and three days later
I got a call from George Clooney’s partner

00:06:33.999 --> 00:06:38.039
asking if he could option it and I said “How
did you hear about that?”

00:06:38.039 --> 00:06:39.589
And he said “I can’t tell you.”

00:06:39.589 --> 00:06:45.460
And I said “Well…if you can’t tell me…do
you want to read it?

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:46.460
What do you want to do?”

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.900
And he goes “No…no…we’ve read it already
and we want to make an offer on it.”

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:56.270
So that began an auction on that property
too.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:01.229
But I learned that there was a wormhole going
on because I called the editor and fished

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:05.740
around talking to her and she denied that
anyone had ever read it outside of her office

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:13.569
which couldn’t have been true because clearly
someone had read it, got snuck out through

00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:14.689
the wormhole.

00:07:14.689 --> 00:07:20.129
I thought I could only have done this if I
had the whole mentality of I will not find

00:07:20.129 --> 00:07:25.469
my niche if I’m only working in films, I
will not only work in books, I will work in

00:07:25.469 --> 00:07:27.409
both and love them both.

00:07:27.409 --> 00:07:33.629
They are just two different forms of storytelling
and why can’t any story that’s dramatic

00:07:33.629 --> 00:07:38.729
end up in both camps and that’s kind of
what I’ve done throughout my career is try

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:40.559
to get the story for both camps.

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:45.249
Film Courage: It’s funny too because I would
think the entrance into the literary world

00:07:45.249 --> 00:07:51.509
(the New York literary world) is much more
based on pedigree and different things, whereas

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:53.740
Hollywood…you’ve got a great script?

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.990
Anything goes, you’re in the front door
if they like you.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:00.919
So it sounds like that’s not always the
case, that’s my perception of it.

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:11.219
Dr. Ken Atchity: Without offending you, it’s
an old fashioned, 1950’s perception.

00:08:11.219 --> 00:08:14.679
Because you can have a great script and get
no where in Hollywood.

00:08:14.679 --> 00:08:19.149
It’s getting it to the right person that
matter.

00:08:19.149 --> 00:08:25.060
And even with a great script as that (where
I talked the guy into writing the book) and

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:28.349
then sold it to an auction too.

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:33.620
He had a great script and he was able to get
it to people but they didn’t buy it because

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:40.720
what’s happened in the last 20 years is
that Hollywood and New York have become corporatized.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:48.200
They’ve all been acquired by international
conglomerate and there isn’t a single studio

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:55.672
that isn’t owned by some foreign accumulation
with the exception of Disney which is of course

00:08:55.672 --> 00:08:58.870
itself an international conglomerate, right?

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:05.310
But Sony owns Columbia and Paramount is owned
by CBS, Viacom.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:11.340
So because of that the executives have totally
changed from a world in which (and the same

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:17.630
is true in publishing) from a world people
with guts and vision made decisions about

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:21.390
doing the story, publishing it or green lighting
a movie.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:29.520
Now it’s corporate people wearing suits
who are very worried for their jobs, who I

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:37.070
alway say their main focus is on their gold
card and their Mercedes and they don’t want

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:39.300
to do anything to jeopardize that.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:43.780
Whereas the heads of studios in the old days
would just take changes “This is a great

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:44.780
story.

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:48.240
I love it, I’m going to do this story.”

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.530
But now they have to show marketing reports
(and this is true in New York just as well)

00:09:53.530 --> 00:10:01.010
because Hachette has bought the old Warner
books and CBS owns Simon &amp; Schuster, Touchstone

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:03.250
and all those things.

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:08.880
Every one of the big companies, Holtzbrinck
owns Macmillan, Thomas Dunne Books and Tor

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:17.820
Books and St. Martin's Press and Penguin Doubleday,
Random House, they are all part of one huge

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.820
foreign conglomerate (and so on).

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:26.780
So because of that every one has to think
like corporate employees.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:33.850
They have to provide marketing marketing P&amp;L’s
to the editorial department and the marketing

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:34.850
department.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:38.600
The marketing department has the last say
not the editorial department.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:42.350
They go “How do you know we’re going to
sell 100,000 copies of this book?”

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:44.020
“Well I just have a gut feeling.”

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:48.620
“Well sorry, your tract record doesn’t
justify you having a gut feeling.”

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:53.320
So they’ll let a really talented, brilliant
editor make a couple of decisions like that

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:54.710
a year.

00:10:54.710 --> 00:11:01.490
If she doesn’t prove it out by those books
becoming best-sellers, she gets less leash

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:04.670
every year and the same is true in Hollywood.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:09.990
And the studios have gotten even worse because
they are focused now entirely on brands and

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:15.270
in the last six years or so they are now doing
a fraction of the number of movies they used

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:22.970
to do because they’d rather spend two hundred
million dollars on a brand then take a 50

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:27.510
million dollar chance on a great story that
has no track record.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:30.590
That’s why TWILIGHT got kicked out of Paramount.

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:37.160
It was sitting there in development hell for
several years and Paramount didn’t get it

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:41.150
and they didn’t understand why it was going
to be great.

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:47.320
Someone came along and bought it from Paramount
and cashed out on it and Paramount goes “Oh?

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:48.320
Okay, well…”

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.670
They didn’t have any regrets because there
is no they there.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:59.700
No one sits around wringing hands, they are
all doing understandable logic and no one

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:03.020
lost their job over it, that’s the important
part.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:08.330
Whereas if you green light a movie and it
goes down the tubes, you can lose your job.

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:16.060
Then you’re out shopping for a new Mercedes
at another place.

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:18.960
So that’s kind of what’s happened to our
world.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:25.660
In the meantime, the need for stories continues
and storytellers are still hugely in demand,

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:31.870
you just have to find a new path each time,
that’s why I started the Imprint so I could

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:37.780
get around the issue and then I just take
to New York big brands like something with

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:45.140
the word Kennedy on it or Dracula and I can
sell those still but I know I can’t sell

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:46.840
smaller books.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:53.320
I try sometimes because I love the books so
much but invariably it comes down to we better

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:53.620
publish this ourselves.

